<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">S. N. Goenka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/2013">2013</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Satya Narayan Goenka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">S. N. Goenka was a Burmese-born Indian teacher of vipassana meditation. Born into an Indian business family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, he learned meditation from the lay teacher Sayagyi U Ba Khin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> and from 1969 taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His ten-day silent retreats spread worldwide, and the organisation he founded runs more than 200 meditation centres.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Satya Narayana Goenka was born on 30 January 1924 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> to Indian parents of the Marwari Bania community and grew up in a conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> household. He was a successful businessman when, in 1955, he began to suffer severe, debilitating migraines for which he found no medical relief. On a friend's suggestion he met the Vipassana teacher Sayagyi U Ba Khin, who was at first reluctant but took him as a student. Goenka trained under him for 14 years.</p><p>Goenka later said that had his teacher asked him to perform rites or rituals he would have left, since his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition was full of them. U Ba Khin told him that the Buddha taught only sila, samadhi and paññā. Goenka retired from business in 1962, when the military government nationalised business in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1969 U Ba Khin authorised Goenka to teach, and U Ba Khin died in 1971. Goenka left his business to his family and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he began his first centre at Kusum Nagar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. In 1976 he opened Dhamma Giri at Igatpuri near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. He taught alone until 1982, then began training assistant teachers, and in 1985 he established the Vipassana Research Institute at Dhamma Giri. From the start he taught intensive retreats of ten days.</p><p>In 2000 he laid the foundation of the Global Vipassana Pagoda near Gorai Beach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, which opened in 2009 and holds relics of the Buddha. It was built as a tribute to U Ba Khin, who wanted to repay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s debt as the land where Vipassana began but never obtained a passport. On 29 August 2000 Goenka spoke at the Millennium World Peace Summit of Religious and Spiritual Leaders at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He wrote in English and Hindi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> awarded him the Padma Bhushan in 2012.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Goenka died on 29 September 2013 at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, survived by his wife Elaichi Devi Goenka and six sons. He trained about 1,350 assistant teachers, who conduct courses using his recordings. Courses are free, with no charge for teaching, food or lodging, and are funded by donations from earlier students. He presented the technique as non-sectarian and open to people of all faiths or none, beginning with observation of the natural breath and moving to observing bodily sensations with equanimity. Jack Kornfield and Jay Michaelson wrote that he was a core teacher for the first generation of insight meditation teachers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “S. N. Goenka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Burmese Buddhist scholarship and meditation teachers, notably Mahasi Sayadaw and the lay teacher S.N. <b>Goenka</b>, who trained in Burma, spread vipassana meditation worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.98,96.08&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Mandalay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7126 1352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of S. N. Goenka"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7576 1622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7576 1622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.98,96.08&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
